✨ 𝗧𝗲𝗿𝗿𝗮𝗳𝗼𝗿𝗺 𝗧𝗶𝗽 𝗼𝗳 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗗𝗮𝘆! 🚀
When working with Terraform, a map is a key-value pair data structure that helps manage and organize related values more cleanly.

✅ 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝗮 𝗺𝗮𝗽?
In Terraform, a map lets you store values that can be looked up using a key. 

𝗙𝗼𝗿 𝗲𝘅𝗮𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲:
variable "vm_tags" {
   type = map(string)
   default = {
    environment = "dev"
    owner    = "shruti"
   }
}

✅ 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝘂𝘀𝗲 𝗮 𝗺𝗮𝗽?
Maps are extremely useful when you want to manage configurations for multiple similar resources without repeating code. They improve readability, scalability, and flexibility.

🚩 𝗖𝗼𝗺𝗺𝗼𝗻 𝗜𝘀𝘀𝘂𝗲 𝘄𝗶𝘁𝗵 𝗳𝗼𝗿_𝗲𝗮𝗰𝗵 + 𝗹𝗶𝘀𝘁 
Many beginners hit a snag when creating multiple resources with for_each and a list — 
for example, creating multiple 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗼𝘂𝗿𝗰𝗲 𝗚𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗽𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝗱𝗶𝗳𝗳𝗲𝗿𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗹𝗼𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀.

👉 With a list, you can't directly associate each Resource Group with a different location.
💥 𝗜𝘀𝘀𝘂𝗲: You can’t specify a different location for each Resource Group.

✅ 𝗦𝗼𝗹𝘂𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻: 𝗨𝘀𝗲 𝗳𝗼𝗿_𝗲𝗮𝗰𝗵 + 𝗺𝗮𝗽 (Refer the image)
Use a 𝗺𝗮𝗽 instead of a list — this way, you can associate each Resource Group with a custom location.
🎉 Now, each Resource Group has its own location — clean and flexible!

